Hi.

something like when you know what you want to do.

for INLA's part, you do not need to ``impute missing values''. its only
for the inferencial part, in the case where the missingness is
informative in itself.

> And purely pragmatically how does one tell inla()
> that specific age values (say 1,12,16) are missing from the observed
> data - I've not seen any example to disect/learn from. (Is there a
> particular text book you might recommend ?)

this is very case spesific and there is no general answer.

> > > And finally I'm still wondering could I use multiply imputed
> > data
> > > from the mice package ?
> >
> > you can always use imputed data as input, as long as they are
> > constant.
>
> Constant meaning ?
> My plan was for example to impute 10 times using the mice package -
> giving 10 datasets -build the same inla model for each dataset - then
> simply sample from the posterior probability of each model and
> combine the estimates for summary statistics.
>

yes, you can impute say '10 times'. for each of these 10, run inla(),
and then merge the posteriors.

